# Overwatch 2 Anti-Cheat Architecture: Eidolon + Warden — Detailed Analysis

> Compiled 2026-10-30. Based on the UnKnoWnCheaTs thread "[Information] Full Eidolon & Warden Analysis"
> (thread 761091, by `nohtinginLife`, 6 July 2026, 54 replies), its related threads,
> and further online research (Blizzard/Activision official statements, community reversal work).

---

## 1. Executive Summary

Overwatch 2 (PC, Battle.net) is protected by a **two-layer, fully user-mode anti-cheat**:

- **Layer 1 — Eidolon (`Overwatch_Loader.dll`)**: an anti-tamper layer sitting between
  `Overwatch.exe` and the Windows API. Responsible for encrypted IAT, string decryption,
  code obfuscation (control-flow flattening), HWID collection, and integrity verification.
- **Layer 2 — Warden (inside `Overwatch.exe`)**: runtime anti-cheat enforcement using
  Vectored Exception Handling (VEH), INT3 trampoline patches, timing checks, process
  enumeration, and integrity scanning modules.

The most important architectural finding: **`Overwatch.exe` has exactly one static import —
`Overwatch_Loader.dll!eidolon_run`**, with zero direct Win32 imports. Every Windows API call
is routed through the loader's encrypted IAT. Consequence: any detour must hook either the
loader's resolved IAT or operate at the syscall level; hooking the game's IAT directly is not
possible because there is nothing to hook statically.

Warden is Blizzard's long-standing anti-cheat brand (used in Diablo II since patch 1.11,
StarCraft 1.15, WarCraft III, World of Warcraft, and Overwatch). Per the UC wiki, Warden
collects data on open processes on the user's machine and sends hash values to Blizzard
servers, which are compared against known cheat signatures. Overwatch 2 continues this model
in user mode, **without a kernel driver** (unlike Riot Vanguard or Activision Ricochet).
Blizzard's public "Defense Matrix" initiative (Aug 2024, Season 12) reports >500,000 accounts
banned for cheating and >40,000 accounts suspended for grouping with cheaters.

